%[text] %[text:anchor:T_DEF03274] # Learning Fuel Cells Through Data: An IoT and Machine Learning Lab 
%[text] 
%[text] The global energy landscape is rapidly shifting toward low‑carbon, electrified, and intelligent energy systems. Fuel cells, particularly **Proton Exchange Membrane Fuel Cells (PEMFCs)**, can play a critical role in this transition due to their relatively high efficiency, fast dynamic response, and suitability for integration with renewable energy sources.
%[text] At the same time, modern energy systems are no longer evaluated solely based on thermodynamic performance. **Real-time monitoring, data analytics, digital twins, and machine learning** have become tools of great interest and use to improve reliability, safety, and economic viability. Engineers are increasingly expected to:
%[text] - Acquire data from physical systems using sensors and IoT platforms
%[text] - Build **data-driven models**
%[text] - Perform system **monitoring**
%[text] - Use **AI-assisted tools responsibly** to accelerate modeling, analysis, and experimentation \
%[text] Data-driven models have become particularly valuable because they can capture system behavior directly from measured data without requiring a complete understanding of all underlying physical phenomena. In fuel cell systems, where electrochemical, thermal, mass transport, and fluid dynamics interact in complex ways, purely physics-based models can be computationally intensive, costly and difficult to calibrate. Data-driven approaches can complement these models by providing efficient approximations of system behavior across a range of operating conditions.
%[text] Furthermore, data-driven models can be integrated with physics-based models to create **hybrid models**. In such frameworks, machine learning algorithms can be used for parameter estimation, model calibration, uncertainty reduction, and adaptation to changing operating conditions. Hybrid modeling combines the interpretability and physical consistency of mechanistic models with the flexibility and predictive power of data-driven techniques to make it especially attractive for fuel cell performance prediction to use in optimization and control applications.
%[text] Another important advantage of data-driven models is their computational efficiency. Compared with high-fidelity electrochemical modeling, reduced-order machine learning models often require substantially less computational power to enable real-time execution on embedded processors, microcontrollers, and edge computing devices. These lightweight models support applications such as real-time system monitoring, predictive maintenance, fault detection, model predictive control, and hardware-in-the-loop simulations. As intelligent energy systems increasingly rely on distributed sensing and decision-making, the ability to deploy accurate models at the edge becomes a significant practical advantage.
%[text] This laboratory module brings these ideas together using a small scale **PEM fuel cell test bench** as a realistic, small-scale plant model that mirrors challenges encountered in real-world clean energy systems.
